Step 1: Detect the cycle
The slow pointer moves one step at a time using nums[slow].
The fast pointer moves two steps at a time using nums[nums[fast]].
When both pointers meet, a cycle has been detected.

Step 2: Find the duplicate
After detecting the cycle, I reset slow to index 0 while keeping fast at the meeting point.
Then I move both pointers one step at a time. The index where they meet again corresponds to the duplicate number.


class Solution {
    public int findDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        int slow=0,fast=0;

        while(true){
            slow=nums[slow];
            fast=nums[nums[fast]];

            if(slow==fast){
                slow=0;
                while(slow!=fast){
                    slow=nums[slow];
                    fast=nums[fast];
                }
                return slow;
            }
        }
    }
}
